## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design multi-tenant inference with dynamic schemas, deterministic preprocessing, and zero-downtime schema updates.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped schema registry with versioning
- Runtime feature alignment and type casting
- Per-tenant batching and routing to model versions
- Graceful schema hot-reload and in-flight request integrity
- gRPC path to TF Serving and error handling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: load tenant schema and map fields to tensor order
function preprocess(input, tenantId, version){
  const schema = registry.get(tenantId, version);
  const ordered = schema.fields.map(f => input[f] ?? schema.defaults[f]);
  // cast types, handle missing, etc.
  return tf.tensor(ordered);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor tail latency per-tenant and detect schema drift in this setup?
- What consistency model would you enforce for schema updates across replicas?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design multi-model serving with isolation, routing, and canary updates.

## Key Concepts
- TensorFlow Serving ModelConfig and signatures
- Request routing via metadata headers
- BatchConfig tuning per model
- GPU isolation with Kubernetes quotas
- Canary rollout and observability

## Code Example
```python
# Minimal routing sketch for gateway by X-Model-Name
def route_request(model_name, payload):
    if model_name == 'text_classifier':
        return run_text_classifier(payload)
    elif model_name == 'mt_model':
        return run_mt_model(payload)
    elif model_name == 'video_analyzer':
        return run_video_analyzer(payload)
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate canary deployments with real traffic?
- What metrics define success for per-model latency budgets?
- How would you detect and respond to model drift across models?
